U = [
 {"p": 1,
  "w": "ཟབ་ཆོས་ ཞི་ཁྲོ་ དགོངས་པ་ རང་གྲོལ་ ལས༔ ཆོས་ཉིད་ བར་དོའི་ གསལ་འདེབས་ ཐོས་གྲོལ་ ཆེན་མོ་ ཞེས་ བྱ་བ་ བཞུགས་སོ༔",
  "l": "Profound-Teaching, Peaceful-and-Wrathful, Mind's Self-Liberation, from: Reality-Itself, of-the-between, the making-clear, Hearing-Liberation, Great, so called, is-here.",
  "i": "From The Profound Teaching, The Peaceful and Wrathful Ones: Natural Liberation of the Mind. Here is the reminder for the bardo of reality, called The Great Liberation through Hearing.",
  "n": "This is the title leaf, and it shows how a Tibetan sentence is built: everything comes before the word it belongs to, and the verb comes last. bzhugs so, 'is present', is the respectful way a title page says 'here is'. las, 'from', names the larger cycle this text belongs to, the Zhi khro dgongs pa rang grol, a collection of 'treasure' texts that Karma Lingpa is said to have brought out of a mountain in the fourteenth century. Thos grol, 'liberation by hearing', is the name Tibetans use for the book: bar do thos grol, Bardo Thödröl. 'The Tibetan Book of the Dead' is the title given to the first English version in 1927. bar do is 'between two': the interval between one state and the next, above all between death and rebirth. The mark ༔, two small circles, closes each phrase. Ordinary Tibetan books use a plain stroke ། there; the circles are kept for treasure texts."},

 {"p": 1, "v": 1,
  "w": "ཆོས་སྐུ་ སྣང་བ་མཐའ་ཡས་ འོད་དཔག་མེད༔ / ལོངས་སྤྱོད་རྫོགས་སྐུ་ པདྨ་ ཞི་ཁྲོའི་ ལྷ༔ / པདྨ་འབྱུང་གནས་ སྤྲུལ་སྐུ་ འགྲོ་བའི་ མགོན༔ / སྐུ་གསུམ་ བླ་མ་ རྣམས་ ལ་ ཕྱག་འཚལ་ལོ༔",
  "l": "Truth-body, Boundless-Appearance, Measureless-Light;\nperfect-enjoyment-body, the Lotus peaceful-and-wrathful's gods;\nLotus-Born, emanation-body, of-beings the protector:\nthree-bodies lamas (plural) to, I-bow.",
  "i": "To the body of truth, Amitābha of boundless light;\nto the body of perfect enjoyment, the peaceful and wrathful deities of the Lotus family;\nto the body of emanation, Padmasambhava, protector of beings:\nto the lamas of the three bodies I bow.",
  "n": "Every Tibetan religious text opens with a homage. This one is in verse, nine syllables to the line, and the verse runs on for fifteen lines before the prose starts. A buddha is said to have three bodies (sku gsum): the body of truth (chos sku), which is reality itself and has no form; the body of enjoyment (longs spyod rdzogs sku), the radiant form seen in visions; and the body of emanation (sprul sku, the word behind 'tulku'), a form in flesh and blood. Here each is given a face: Amitābha, whose two Tibetan names are both in the first line; the hundred peaceful and wrathful deities, who will appear to the dead person later in the book; and Padmasambhava, 'the Lotus-Born', the Indian master of the eighth century to whom the book is credited. The verb waits until the very end: phyag 'tshal lo, 'I bow'. The -lo is a full stop you can hear: Tibetan closes a statement by repeating the verb's last consonant with -o."},

 {"v": 1,
  "w": "རྣལ་འབྱོར་ འབྲིང་ རྣམས་ བར་དོར་ གྲོལ་བའི་ ཐབས༔ / ཐོས་གྲོལ་ ཆེན་མོ་ འདི་ ལ་ དོན་ གསུམ་ སྟེ༔ / སྔོན་འགྲོ་ དངོས་གཞི་ རྗེས་ དང་ གསུམ་ ཡིན་ནོ༔",
  "l": "Yogins middling (plural), in-the-between being-freed's means:\nHearing-Liberation Great this, in, parts three, namely:\npreliminary, main-part, after, and, three they-are.",
  "i": "This Great Liberation through Hearing, the means by which yogins of middling ability are freed in the bardo, has three parts: the preliminaries, the main part and the conclusion.",
  "n": "An adjective follows its noun (rnal 'byor 'bring, 'yogins middling'; thos grol chen mo, 'hearing-liberation great'), and so do 'this' and the plural marker rnams. bar dor is bar do with -r, 'in' or 'into', fused onto it; grol ba'i is grol ba, 'being freed', with the 'of' ending -'i, so that the whole phrase before thabs describes the 'means'. You will see both endings constantly. The 'middling' are those who were not freed in life by meditation; the next lines set out the three chances a person has."},

 {"v": 1,
  "w": "དང་པོ་ སྔོན་འགྲོའི་ ལུས་ཅན་ གྲོལ་བ་ ནི༔ / ཁྲིད་ ཀྱི་ རིམ་པ་ ཐོག་མར་ ཉམས་སུ་བླངས༔ / དབང་པོ་ རབ་ རྣམས་ ཁྲིད་ ཀྱིས་ ངེས་པར་ གྲོལ༔",
  "l": "First, the preliminary's embodied-ones being-freed, as-for:\nguidance's stages at-the-first into-experience taken;\nfaculties best (plural), by guidance certainly freed.",
  "i": "First, the preliminaries, by which embodied beings are freed. The stages of guidance should be practised first; those of the highest ability are sure to be freed by the guidance alone.",
  "n": "The print seems to read dus can here, which gives no sense; other prints have lus can, 'those who have a body', and that is the text given. ni marks the topic, 'as for'. kyi is 'of' and kyis is 'by': the same particle with -s added marks the doer or the means, and both change their first letter to suit the word before (kyi, gyi, gi, 'i, yi). nyams su blangs, 'taken into experience', is the ordinary expression for putting a teaching into practice. The 'guidance' (khrid) is a lama's step-by-step instruction in meditation during life."},

 {"v": 1,
  "w": "དེས་ མ་ གྲོལ་ ན་ འཆི་ཁའི་ བར་དོ་ ལ༔ / འཕོ་བ་ དྲན་པ་ རང་གྲོལ་ ཉམས་སུ་བླངས༔ / དེས་ ནི་ རྣལ་འབྱོར་ འབྲིང་ རྣམས་ ངེས་པར་ གྲོལ༔",
  "l": "By-that not freed if, death-point's between in:\nthe Transference, Remembering Self-Freed, into-experience taken;\nby-that, yogins middling (plural) certainly freed.",
  "i": "If that does not free them, then in the bardo of the moment of death they should practise the transference called Liberation upon Remembering; by that, yogins of middling ability are sure to be freed.",
  "n": "ma is 'not' and stands before the verb; na, 'if', stands after it: des ma grol na, 'if not freed by that'. This phrase comes three times. 'pho ba, 'moving across', is the practice of sending one's consciousness out through the crown of the head at the moment of death. 'Pho ba dran pa rang grol is the title of a separate text of the same cycle."},

 {"v": 1,
  "w": "དེས་ མ་ གྲོལ་ ན་ ཆོས་ཉིད་ བར་དོ་ ལ༔ / ཐོས་གྲོལ་ ཆེན་མོ་ འདི་ ལ་ ནན་ཏན་ བྱའོ༔",
  "l": "By-that not freed if, reality-itself between in:\nHearing-Liberation Great this, at, earnest-effort is-to-be-done.",
  "i": "If that does not free them, then in the bardo of reality they should apply themselves in earnest to this Great Liberation through Hearing.",
  "n": "bya'o is bya, 'to be done', closed with the sentence-ending -'o. Most instructions in this book end with it: 'is to be done', that is, 'one should'. The chos nyid bar do, the bardo of reality itself, is the stretch after death in which the peaceful and wrathful deities appear."},

 {"p": 1,
  "w": "དེ་ ལ་ དང་པོ་ རྣལ་འབྱོར་པས༔ འཆི་ བརྟག་པ་ རྣམས་ འཆི་ལྟས་ གསལ་བའི་ མེ་ལོང་ ལྟར་ བརྟག་པར་ བྱའོ༔",
  "l": "In that, first, by-the-yogin: death examinations (plural), Death-Signs' Making-Clear Mirror according-to, to-examine is-to-be-done.",
  "i": "To begin with, the yogin should examine himself for the approach of death, in the way set out in The Mirror That Makes Clear the Signs of Death.",
  "n": "The prose begins. rnal 'byor pas is 'the yogin' with -s, 'by': the doer is marked, as it will be in every sentence that says who does something. In this print the companion text is called 'Chi ltas gsal ba'i me long; other prints of the book call it 'Chi ltas mtshan ma rang grol, 'Natural Liberation of the Marks and Signs of Death'. It lists the bodily signs and dreams by which death can be foreseen."},

 {"w": "དེས་ན་ ངེས་པར་ འཆི་བའི་ རྟགས་ རྣམས་ ཚང་བའི་ ཚེ༔ འཕོ་བ་ དྲན་པ་ རང་གྲོལ་ བཏབ་པར་ བྱའོ༔",
  "l": "So, certainly dying's signs (plural) being-complete's time: the Transference, Remembering Self-Freed, to-apply is-to-be-done.",
  "i": "Then, when the signs of certain death are all present, the transference, Liberation upon Remembering, should be performed.",
  "n": "btab is the past of 'debs, a verb you will meet in almost every line that follows. Its plain sense is 'to cast, to plant, to strike'; it is the verb for saying a prayer, setting a seal, and here for carrying out a practice or giving an instruction to the dying. gdab is its future, 'to be given'."},

 {"w": "དེས་ན་ འཕོ་བ་ ཐེབས་ ན་ ཐོས་གྲོལ་ ཀློག་ མི་ དགོས་སོ༔",
  "l": "So, the transference took-hold if, the Hearing-Liberation to-read not is-needed.",
  "i": "If the transference takes effect, there is no need to read the Liberation through Hearing.",
  "n": "thebs, 'to land, to take hold', is the result of 'debs: the practice was cast and it struck home. mi is the 'not' used before present and future verbs, ma before past ones."},

 {"w": "གལ་ཏེ་ འཕོ་བ་ མ་ ཐེབ་ ན་ ཐོས་གྲོལ་ འདི་ རོའི་ རྩར་ སྒྲ་ དག་ ལ་ ཚིག་ གསལ་བར་ ཀློག་པར་ བྱའོ༔",
  "l": "If the transference not took-hold if, Hearing-Liberation this, the corpse's at-the-side, sound correct and words clearly, to-read is-to-be-done.",
  "i": "If the transference has not taken effect, this Liberation through Hearing should be read beside the body, with correct pronunciation and every word distinct.",
  "n": "gal te ... na brackets the condition: 'if' at both ends. After dag, la means 'and': following a verb or adjective it links one clause to the next. Much rests on the reading being clear, because the dead person is taken to be listening."},

 {"w": "རོ་ མེད་ ན་ ཚེ་འདས་ ཀྱི་ མལ་སའམ་ འདུག་སར་ བསྡད་ ལ༔ བདེན་པའི་ སྟོབས་ བརྗོད་ ལ་ རྣམ་ཤེས་ བཀུག་ ནས༔ མདུན་དུ་ ཉན་པར་ དམིགས་ ཤིང་ ཀློག་པར་ བྱའོ༔",
  "l": "Corpse there-is-not if, the life-passed-one's bed-or sitting-place-on having-sat, and: truth's power having-spoken, and, the consciousness having-summoned, then: in-front listening imagining, and, to-read is-to-be-done.",
  "i": "If there is no body, the reader should sit on the dead person's bed or seat, call on the power of truth, summon the consciousness, picture it in front of him listening, and read.",
  "n": "A chain of actions, each verb tied to the next by a small linking word (la, nas, shing), with the main verb at the end. This is how Tibetan prose moves, and the book is full of it. tshe 'das, 'one whose life has passed', is the polite word for the dead. -'am is 'or', written onto the end of mal sa. 'The power of truth' is a solemn declaration that calls on the truth of the Buddha's teaching to bring something about."},

 {"w": "འདིའི་ དུས་ ཉེ་དུ་ དང་ སེམས་ ལ་ བརྩེ་བའི་ མཛའ་བོ་ ཚོས་ ངུ་སྐད་ དང་༔ སྨྲེ་སྔགས་ ཡེ་ནས་ འདོན་དུ་ མི་ རུང་བས་ དམ་ བསྒྲགས་པའོ༔",
  "l": "This's time, relatives and in-heart loving friends (plural, by): weeping-sound and wailing at-all to-utter not being-fitting-because, strictly it-is-proclaimed.",
  "i": "At this time relatives and close friends must not weep or wail aloud at all, and this is to be strictly announced.",
  "n": "tshos is the everyday plural tsho with -s, 'by', where the book has so far used the literary plural rnams. Such touches of spoken Tibetan are part of the book's character: it was written to be used at a deathbed. Weeping is forbidden because the dead person hears it and is pulled back toward the life just left."},

 {"p": 1,
  "w": "རོ་ ཡོད་ ན་ ཕྱི་དབུགས་ ཆད་པའི་ མཚམས་སུ་ བླ་མའམ་ མཆེད་གྲོགས་སམ༔ དད་པ་ ཐོབ་པ༔ ཡིད་ མཐུན་པའི་ གྲོགས་མཆེད་ ལ་སོགས་པས༔ རྣ་བར་ མཆུ་ མ་ རེག་ ཙམ་དུ་ གཏད་ ལ༔ ཐོས་གྲོལ་ ཆེན་མོ་ འདི་ ཀློག་པར་ བྱའོ༔",
  "l": "Corpse there-is if, the outer-breath having-stopped's juncture-at, a lama-or a dharma-companion-or, one-who-won trust, in-mind agreeing friend-companion, and-so-on-by: to-the-ear the lips not touching just-so having-aimed, and: Hearing-Liberation Great this to-read is-to-be-done.",
  "i": "If the body is there, then at the point when the outer breath has stopped, a lama, or a fellow disciple, or someone the dead person trusted, a like-minded friend or the like, should put his lips close to the ear, just short of touching it, and read this Great Liberation through Hearing.",
  "n": "'Or' is the last consonant of the word repeated with -am: bla ma'am, mched grogs sam, and further on gsum mam and bdun nam. phyi dbugs, the 'outer breath', is breathing as an onlooker sees it. The book holds that an 'inner breath' goes on for a while after it stops, and that this interval is the moment to speak."},

 {"p": 1,
  "w": "དེ་ ལ་ ཐོས་གྲོལ་ དངོས་ བཤད་པ་ ནི༔ དཀོན་མཆོག་གསུམ་ ལ་ མཆོད་པ་ རྒྱ་ཆེན་པོ་ འབུལ༔",
  "l": "In that, the Hearing-Liberation itself explaining, as-for: Rare-Supreme-Three to, offering vast offer.",
  "i": "Now for the Liberation through Hearing itself. Make a lavish offering to the Three Jewels.",
  "n": "dkon mchog gsum, 'the three rare and supreme ones', is the Tibetan for the Three Jewels: the Buddha, his teaching and the community."},

 {"w": "མ་ འབྱོར་ ན་ དམིགས་པའི་ རྟེན་ གང་ འབྱོར་ བཤམས་ ལ༔ ཡིད་ ཀྱིས་ དཔག་ཏུ་མེད་པ་ སྤྲུལ་ ལ་ མཆོད༔",
  "l": "Not within-means if, imagining's supports whatever is-at-hand having-set-out, and: by-mind immeasurable having-created, and, offer.",
  "i": "If that is beyond your means, set out whatever you have to hand as a support for the imagination, create immeasurable offerings in the mind, and offer those.",
  "n": "sprul, 'to create by the mind's power', is the verb inside sprul sku, the 'body of emanation' of the opening verse."},

 {"w": "དེ་ནས་ སངས་རྒྱས་ དང་ བྱང་ཆུབ་སེམས་དཔའ་ རྣམས་ ལ་ ར་མདའ་ སྦྲན་པའི་ སྨོན་ལམ་ འདི་ ལན་ གསུམ་མམ་ བདུན་ དུ་ འདོན༔",
  "l": "Then, buddhas and bodhisattvas (plural) to, help calling's prayer this, times three-or seven, recite.",
  "i": "Then recite three or seven times the prayer Calling on the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas for Help.",
  "n": "The first of four short verse prayers that are said before the reading. They are separate texts of the same cycle and stand in this volume after the Great Liberation. sangs rgyas, 'awakened and unfolded', is the Tibetan for 'buddha'; byang chub sems dpa', 'hero whose mind is set on awakening', for 'bodhisattva'."},

 {"w": "དེ་ནས་ བར་དོ་ འཇིགས་སྐྱོབ་མའི་ སྨོན་ལམ་ དང་༔ བར་དོ་ འཕྲང་སྒྲོལ་ གྱི་ སྨོན་ལམ་ དང་༔ བར་དོའི་ རྩ་ཚིག་ རྣམས་ དབྱངས་ དང་ བཅས་ཏེ་ རྣ་བར་ བཏབ༔",
  "l": "Then, the Between Fear-Protecting's Prayer, and, the Between Narrow-Passage-Delivering's Prayer, and, the Between's Root-Verses (plural), melody with together, into-the-ear cast.",
  "i": "Then chant into the ear the Prayer for Protection from Fear in the Bardo, the Prayer for Deliverance from the Narrow Passage of the Bardo, and the Root Verses of the Bardo.",
  "n": "dang, 'and', follows each item of a list, and dang bcas te, 'together with', closes it: 'with melody', that is, chanted. 'phrang is a narrow, dangerous path along a cliff, the image the book uses for the passage through death."},

 {"p": 1,
  "w": "དེ་ནས་ ཐོས་གྲོལ་ ཆེན་མོ་ འདི་ ལན་ བདུན་ནམ་ གསུམ་ སྐབས་ དང་ སྦྱར་ ལ་ ཀློག་པ་ ནི༔",
  "l": "Then, Hearing-Liberation Great this, times seven-or three, the occasion with having-fitted, and, reading, as-for:",
  "i": "Then read this Great Liberation through Hearing seven times or three, as the occasion allows. The reading is as follows."},

 {"w": "འཆི་ཁའི་ བར་དོ་ ལ་ འོད་གསལ་ ངོ་སྤྲོད་པ་ དང་ ཆོས་ཉིད་ བར་དོ་ ལ་ ངོ་སྤྲོད་ གསལ་འདེབས་ ཆེན་མོ་ གདབ་པ་ དང་༔ སྲིད་པ་ བར་དོ་ ལ་ མངལ་སྒོ་ དགག་ཐབས་ བསྟན་པ་ དང་ གསུམ་མོ༔",
  "l": "Death-point's between in, the clear-light face-introducing, and; reality-itself between in, the face-introduction, the making-clear great, giving, and; becoming between in, womb-door blocking-means teaching, and: three.",
  "i": "It has three parts: pointing out the clear light in the bardo of the moment of death; giving the great reminder that points things out in the bardo of reality; and teaching the ways of closing the womb-door in the bardo of becoming.",
  "n": "The plan of the whole book. ngo sprod, literally 'to bring face to face', is the key word: to introduce someone to a thing so that he recognises it. A lama 'introduces' a student to the nature of his own mind; the book does the same for the dead at each stage. 'od gsal, 'clear light', is the mind's own nature, said to show itself unclouded at the moment of death. The srid pa bar do, the bardo of becoming, is the last stretch, in which the dead person is drawn toward a new birth: 'closing the womb-door' means preventing it."},

 {"p": 1,
  "w": "དང་པོ་ འཆི་ཁའི་ བར་དོ་ ལ་ འོད་གསལ་ ངོ་སྤྲད་པ་ ནི༔ འདིར་ གང་ཟག་ གོ་བ་ བཟང་ ཡང་ ངོ་ མ་ འཕྲོད་བ་ དང་༔ ངོ་ འཕྲོད་ ཀྱང་ གོམ་འདྲིས་ ཆུང་བ་ རྣམས་ དང་༔ སོ་སོ་སྐྱེ་བོ་ ཉམས་ཁྲིད་ མ་ མྱོང་བའི་ རིགས་ ཐམས་ཅད་ ལ་ འདི་ བཏབ་པས༔",
  "l": "First, death-point's between in, the clear-light face-introduced, as-for: here, persons, understanding good though, face not met, and; face met though, familiarity small (plural), and; ordinary-persons practical-guidance not having-tasted's kinds all, to, this having-been-given-by:",
  "i": "First, pointing out the clear light in the bardo of the moment of death. This is given to people who understand well but have not recognised it, to those who have recognised it but are little practised, and to every kind of ordinary person who has never had practical instruction.",
  "n": "ngo 'phrod, 'the face is met', is what happens when ngo sprod succeeds: the thing pointed out is recognised. yang and kyang are the same word, 'though, even', changing its first letter after different endings. Another print reads zhu myong ba'i, ordinary people 'who have received' instruction, where this one has ma myong ba'i, 'who have not'. The print also writes 'phrod ba and, below, zhus ba, where the rule asks for pa; the spellings are left as printed."},

 {"w": "གཞིའི་ འོད་གསལ་ ངོ་ འཕྲོད་ དེ་ བར་དོ་ མེད་པར་ ཡར་ གྱི་ ཟང་ཐལ་ ལ་ ཆོས་སྐུ་ སྐྱེ་བ་མེད་པ་ ཐོབ་བོ༔",
  "l": "The ground's clear-light, the face having-met, then, a between without, the upward's straight-through by, the truth-body birthless they-attain.",
  "i": "Through it they recognise the clear light of the ground and, with no bardo at all, by the direct way upward, attain the unborn body of truth.",
  "n": "gzhi, 'ground', is the mind as it always is underneath, as against the path walked to reach it. zang thal means passing straight through with nothing in the way; yar gyi zang thal, the 'great straight way up', is the book's expression for being freed at the instant of death without going through any bardo."},

 {"p": 1,
  "w": "གདབ་ལུགས་ ནི་ རང་ གི་ ཁྲིད་ ཞུས་བའི་ རྩ་བའི་ བླ་མ་ འཛོམས་ ན་ ནི་ མཆོག་ ཡིན་ནོ༔",
  "l": "The way-of-giving, as-for: one's-own guidance requested's root lama is-present if, as-for, best it-is.",
  "i": "As to how it is given: it is best if the dying person's own root lama, from whom he received guidance, can be there.",
  "n": "rtsa ba'i bla ma, 'root lama', is one's principal teacher. zhus, 'asked for', is the humble verb for receiving a teaching."},

 {"w": "དེ་ མ་ འཛོམས་ ན་ མཆེད་གྲོགས་ དམ་ཚིག་ གཅིག་པའམ༔ དེ་ ཡང་ མ་ འཛོམས་ ན་ ཆོས་རྒྱུད་ གཅིག་པའི་ བཤེས་གཉེན་པའམ༔ དེ་ རྣམས་ གང་ཡང་ མ་ འཛོམས་ ན༔ ཡི་གེ་ སྒྲ་ དག་ ལ་ ཚིག་ གསལ་བར་ འདོན་ ཤེས་པ་ གཅིག་ གི་ ཚར་ མང་དུ་ ཀློག་གོ༔",
  "l": "He not present if, a dharma-companion, vow the-same, or; he too not present if, teaching-lineage the-same's spiritual-friend, or; those any-at-all not present if: the letters, sound correct and words clearly, to-recite knowing one, by, times many read.",
  "i": "If he cannot, then a fellow disciple bound by the same vows; if not even that, a teacher of the same lineage; and if none of these can be there, someone who knows how to read the text aloud correctly and distinctly should read it many times.",
  "n": "dam tshig is the bond of vows that ties students of one lama to him and to each other. The print has gcig gi, 'of one', where the sense asks for gcig gis, 'by one'; the two are often confused in blockprints, and it is left as printed. klog go: the sentence-ending -o again, after the g of klog."},

 {"w": "དེས་ བླ་མས་ ངོ་སྤྲད་པའི་ དོན་ དྲན་ ནས༔ དེ་མ་ཐག་ཏུ་ གཞིའི་ འོད་གསལ་ ངོ་ འཕྲོད་ དེ་ གྲོལ་བར་ ཐེ་ཚོམ་ མེད་དོ༔",
  "l": "By-that, by-the-lama face-introduced's meaning having-remembered, then: immediately the ground's clear-light, the face having-met, then, to-be-freed doubt there-is-not.",
  "i": "This will bring back to mind what the lama once pointed out, and there is no doubt that the dying person will at once recognise the clear light of the ground and be freed.",
  "n": "The reading only reminds. It works because the lama's introduction was given in life, and that is why the book is called a gsal 'debs, a 'making clear' of something already known."},

 {"p": 1,
  "w": "གདབ་པའི་ དུས་ ནི༔ ཕྱི་དབུགས་ ཆད་ ནས་ རླུང་ ཡེ་ཤེས་ ཀྱི་ དྷཱུ་ཏཱིར་ ཐིམ་པ་ དང་༔ ཤེས་པ་ འོད་གསལ་ སྤྲོས་བྲལ་ དུ་ ལྷག་གིས་ འཆར་རོ༔",
  "l": "The giving's time, as-for: the outer-breath having-stopped, then, the wind wisdom's into-the-dhūtī dissolving, and: awareness, clear-light elaboration-free as, vividly dawns.",
  "i": "As to when it is given: once the outer breath has stopped and the wind has dissolved into the central channel of wisdom, awareness dawns vividly as clear light, free of all elaboration.",
  "n": "The body is pictured as threaded with channels (rtsa) through which winds (rlung) move, carrying the mind. dhūtī is short for the Sanskrit avadhūtī, the central channel, which runs up the middle of the body; the stacked letters are how Tibetan writes Sanskrit sounds it does not have. The length marks on the word are not clear in the scan. spros bral, 'free of elaboration', means free of every idea the mind lays over things."},

 {"w": "དེ་ནས་ རླུང་ ལོག་ སྟེ་ རྩ་ གཡས་ གཡོན་ དུ་ བྲོས་པས་ བར་དོའི་ སྣང་བ་ ལམ་གྱིས་ འཆར་བས༔ རྩ་ གཡས་ གཡོན་ དུ་ མ་ ཤོར་བའི་ བར་ དེར་ གདབ་བོ༔",
  "l": "Then, the wind having-turned-back, the channels right, left, into having-fled-because, the between's appearances in-a-flash dawning-because: the channels right, left, into not having-escaped's interval, in-that, it-is-to-be-given.",
  "i": "After that the wind turns back and escapes into the right and left channels, and the appearances of the bardo flash into view. So it is to be given in the interval before the wind has escaped into the right and left channels.",
  "n": "bar, 'interval', is the first half of bar do. -pas and -bas on a verb mean 'because' or 'and so': the same -s that marks the doer of an action."},

 {"w": "ཡུན་ ནི་ ནང་དབུགས་ གནས་ ཀྱི་ བར་ དེར་ ཟན་ གཅིག་ ཟ་ ཡུན་ ཙམ་ གཅིག་ ཡིན་ནོ༔",
  "l": "The duration, as-for: the inner-breath remaining's interval, in-that, a meal one eating duration about, one, it-is.",
  "i": "As for how long it lasts: while the inner breath remains, about the time it takes to eat a meal.",
  "n": "Another print makes the limits explicit: 'from the stopping of the outer breath for as long as the inner breath remains'."},

 {"p": 1,
  "w": "གདབ་ལུགས་ ནི་ དབུགས་ ཆད་ ལ་ ཁད་པའི་ དུས་ ན་ འཕོ་བ་ ཐེབས་ ན་ རབ༔ མ་ ཐེབ་ ན་ འདི་སྐད་དོ༔",
  "l": "The way-of-giving, as-for: the breath to-stop being-close-to's time at, the transference took-hold if, best; not took-hold if, these-words:",
  "i": "As to how it is given: it is best if the transference takes effect when the breath is on the point of stopping. If it does not, say these words:"},

 {"p": 1,
  "w": "༈ ཀྱཻ༔ རིགས་ཀྱི་བུ་ ཆེ་གེ་མོ་ ཁྱོད་ ད་ ནི་ ལམ་ བཙལ་བའི་ དུས་ ལ་ བབ་པར་ འདུག་པས༔",
  "l": "O! Child of the family, so-and-so, you: now, as-for, the path seeking's time to has-come, it-seems, so:",
  "i": "O child of noble family, (name), the time has now come for you to seek the path.",
  "n": "The first words spoken to the dying person. rigs kyi bu, 'child of the family', translates the Sanskrit kulaputra, the way the Buddha addresses his hearers in the sutras; every address in the book begins with it. che ge mo, 'so-and-so', marks the place where the reader says the person's name. kyai is a call for attention, written with a doubled vowel sign to show the cry drawn out. The curled mark ༈ before it opens a new section. 'dug, 'there is, it appears', belongs to the spoken language."},

 {"w": "ཁྱོད་རང་ དབུགས་ ཆད་ མ་ཁད་དུ་ ཁྱོད་ ལ་ བར་དོ་ དང་པོའི་ འོད་གསལ་ ཞེས་ བྱ་བ་ སྔར་ བླ་མས་ ངོ་སྤྲད་པའི་ དོན་ དེ་ ཕྱི་དབུགས་ ཆད་པ་ དང་༔ ཆོས་ ཐམས་ཅད་ ནམ་མཁའ་ ལྟར་ སྟོང་ རྗེན་རྗེན་པ༔ གསལ་སྟོང་ མཐའ་དབུས་ མེད་པའི་ རིག་པ་ ཕྱིར་ མེད་པ་ རྗེན་པ་ གཅིག་ འཆར་ ནས་ འོང་ངོ༔",
  "l": "You-yourself, the breath stopping as-soon-as, to you the between first's clear-light so called, before by-the-lama face-introduced's meaning, that: the outer-breath stopping with, things all, sky like, empty, bare-bare; clear-empty, edge-centre without's knowing, outside(?) without, bare, one, dawning will-come.",
  "i": "As soon as your breath stops, what is called the clear light of the first bardo, the very thing your lama once pointed out to you, will appear to you. When the outer breath stops, all things are empty and utterly bare, like the sky, and a bare awareness, clear and empty, without edge or centre, will dawn.",
  "n": "One long sentence, held together by its last two words: 'char nas 'ong ngo, 'will come dawning'. rjen is 'naked, raw', and doubling it (rjen rjen pa) strengthens it. rig pa, 'knowing', is the book's word for awareness itself, before any thought. One word is in doubt. The print seems to read phyir med pa, perhaps 'with nothing outside it'; another print has dri med, 'stainless', at this place, and has chos nyid, 'reality itself', where this one has chos thams cad, 'all things'. The print is followed and the translation of that word is a guess."},

 {"w": "དེའི་ དུས་སུ་ ཁྱོད་རང་ གིས་ རང་ངོ་ ཤེས་པར་ གྱིས་ ལ་ དེའི་ ངང་ ལ་ ཞོག་ཅིག༔ ངས་ ཀྱང་ དེའི་ དུས་སུ་ ངོ་སྤྲད་དོ༔",
  "l": "That's time-at, by you-yourself its-own-face to-know do!, and, that's state in, rest! By-me too, that's time-at, the face-is-introduced.",
  "i": "At that moment, recognise it yourself for what it is, and rest in that state. I too will point it out to you at that moment.",
  "n": "Two commands. gyis is 'do!', the imperative of byed, and looks exactly like the 'by' particle just before it (khyod rang gis ... gyis). zhog is 'leave it!, rest!', the imperative of 'jog, and cig after a verb makes the command plain. rang ngo shes pa, 'knowing one's own face', is recognition of one's own nature."},

 {"w": "ཞེས་ ཕྱི་དབུགས་ མ་ ཆད་པའི་ གོང་ནས་ རྣ་བར་ ལན་ མང་དུ་ བཤད་ ལ་ ཡིད་ ལ་ འཇགས་སུ་ བཅུག་གོ༔",
  "l": "So, the outer-breath not having-stopped's before, into-the-ear times many having-said, and, in the mind settled cause-to-be.",
  "i": "Say this in the ear many times before the outer breath has stopped, so that it is fixed in the mind.",
  "n": "zhes closes a quotation, as it closed the title on the first leaf: everything from kyai to here is what the reader says aloud. The book goes on to the moment the breath stops, and to the words to be spoken then."},
]
